      "explanation": "The IMusicPlayer interface defines methods like SkipTrack, Eject, and Shuffle which are physically impossible for a VinylPlayer or an Amplifier. These classes throw NotSupportedException exceptions when these methods are called, violating the Liskov Substitution Principle because code expecting a valid IMusicPlayer (one that performs the action) will break when substituted with a concrete class that throws an exception instead of performing the operation."
    },
    {
      "violation_type": "ISP",
      "explanation": "The IMusicPlayer interface is too large and forces unrelated classes like Amplifier and VinylPlayer to implement methods they cannot logically support (e.g., Eject, Shuffle). This violates the Interface Segregation Principle by forcing clients to depend on interfaces with methods they do not use or need."
    }
  ]
